package cajeta.threading;

import cajeta.lang.Optional;
import cajeta.time.Duration;

/**
 * Task-related utilities. v1 surface: `withTimeout(Duration, Task<R>)` —
 * wait on a previously-spawned task with a deadline, reporting completion
 * vs. timeout via `Optional<R>`.
 *
 * ## API shape
 *
 * The user spawns the task explicitly; withTimeout layers the timeout on
 * top of the existing await machinery:
 *
 *     Task<int32> t = spawn compute();
 *     Optional<int32> r = Tasks.withTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(50), t);
 *     if (r.isPresent()) { ... use r.get() ... } else { ... handle timeout ... }
 *
 * Two forms ship: the templated `withTimeout<R>(Duration, Task<R>) ->
 * #Optional<R>` (the canonical API; R is inferred from the Task<R>
 * arg) and an `int32` specialization `withTimeoutInt32` that predates
 * the generic form and remains for callers already pinned to it. New
 * code should prefer the templated form.
 *
 * A lambda-taking shape — `withTimeoutInt32(d, () -> compute())` per
 * cajeta-docs/stdlib/Thread.md § withTimeout — is the documented
 * eventual API but blocked on `spawn` supporting function-typed
 * callees. v1 of SpawnExpression only handles bare class-method
 * invocations (cajeta-docs/stdlib/AsyncStatus.md § Plan: Task<T>);
 * spawn-of-lambda lands separately. The Task<int32> form covers the
 * use case in the meantime.
 *
 * ## withTimeout semantics (v1 limitations)
 *
 * The function waits on the task's done flag with the supplied deadline.
 * The `Optional<R>` return reports whether the body finished inside the
 * deadline — `present` iff done flipped before the deadline expired,
 * `empty` otherwise.
 *
 * **The body is always awaited to completion before withTimeout returns.**
 * v1 does not cancel the body when the deadline expires; the deadline is a
 * *reporting* boundary, not a *termination* boundary. A slow body still
 * runs to natural completion; the only observable effect of an expired
 * deadline is the `empty` return. This matches the runtime primitive
 * (R9.1 — `__cajeta_task_wait_timeout` waits without cancelling) and
 * avoids the two correctness hazards that v1 isn't ready to solve:
 *
 *   - Returning before the spawn's surrounding scope drop would block the
 *     same scope's exit on the still-running task anyway (scope owns the
 *     task's drop and waits for done before freeing).
 *   - Lambda captures pointing into the caller's stack would dangle if
 *     withTimeout returned with the body still running and the caller
 *     popped its frame.
 *
 * Proper early-return-on-timeout requires fiber cancellation wired through
 * the scope's drop chain — tracked as part of the broader scope+cancel
 * work in R5-C / R9.4. Until that lands, callers who need to interrupt a
 * runaway body must use a cooperative cancellation flag inside the body.
 */
public class Tasks {
    // Returns ownership (heap-allocated Optional) — borrow-return on a
    // multi-parameter free function is disallowed (CAJETA_ERROR_BORROW_RETURN_MULTI_PARAM),
    // since neither parameter is a clean borrow root the return value
    // can be tied to. The heap Optional is then owned by the caller and
    // drops via the usual class drop chain.
    public static #Optional<int32> withTimeoutInt32(Duration d, Task<int32> t) {
        int64 deadline = Cajeta.currentTimeNanos() + d.toNanos();
        pointer done = Cajeta.taskDonePointer(t);
        int32 ok = Cajeta.taskWaitTimeout(done, deadline);
        int32 val = await t;
        if (ok == 1) {
            return heap Optional<int32>(true, val);
        }
        return heap Optional<int32>(false, val);
    }

    public static #Optional<R> withTimeout<R>(Duration d, Task<R> t) {
        int64 deadline = Cajeta.currentTimeNanos() + d.toNanos();
        pointer done = Cajeta.taskDonePointer(t);
        int32 ok = Cajeta.taskWaitTimeout(done, deadline);
        R val = await t;
        if (ok == 1) {
            return heap Optional<R>(true, val);
        }
        return heap Optional<R>(false, val);
    }
}
